 "verdict": "ZERO FORWARD PASSES. wikitext-103 (20M chars), each model's own tokenizer, inflow = Fu et al. Corollary 1.2's term.  K4 COVERAGE: 13 of 15 models readable. Excluded for coverage: [('bloom-3b', ' ciudad', 0)]. Excluded for TOKENIZER LOAD FAILURE, a separate and non-evidential reason: [('polyglot-ko-1.3b', 'OSError')].  K2 TRIVIAL RULE: 'the endpoint is the single most-inflow token' is right for 0 of 13. That rate, not 50%, is the baseline H1 must beat.  H1 (registered, whole-vocabulary percentile): 12 of 13 endpoints are at or above the 90th inflow percentile (median 99.87). H1 SURVIVES on the registered criterion.  H2 (FREQUENCY-MATCHED, the load-bearing test): endpoint inflow beats frequency-matched peers in 1 of 13 models, median percentile 32.0 against 50 by construction. K3 FIRES: inflow adds nothing to frequency. The correct statement is that these maps funnel to COMMON tokens, not to HIGH-INFLOW ones. Fu et al.'s specific claim is UNSUPPORTED on this data; the trivial claim stands untouched. H3 (TIER 2, a NULL): max inflow spans [1320.7, 4684.9] on funnels and [2651.7, 15196.2] on non-funnels. The ranges OVERLAP, so corpus inflow concentration does NOT separate the classes. Stated as registered: this does not PROVE language-independence, it shows this corpus statistic fails to distinguish models that differ sharply in phi -- which is what a weights-side account predicts and a 'caused by the language itself' account does not. NON-INDEPENDENCE, reported not corrected: endpoint tokens repeat across models ({'\\n': 6, '0': 3}), and models sharing a token share its corpus statistics exactly, so the readable models are fewer than 13 independent tests.  REFUSALS, recorded before the numbers: no p-value; no causal claim (corpus bigram inflow is not the model's conditional); no claim that this validates Fu et al.'s BOUND, which is a different object.",
